#: ../../../release_details/9.7/python-gc-traverse.md:1
msgid "Python reference cycles through attributes are now collected"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../release_details/9.7/python-gc-traverse.md:3
msgid ""
"Storing an attribute on a wrapped VTK object no longer risks leaking that "
"object for the lifetime of the process.  The Python wrapper's `tp_traverse` "
"now reports the instance dictionary to CPython's cyclic garbage collector, "
"so a cycle that passes through an attribute -- for example a cached helper "
"that holds the object it was created from -- is found and freed:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../release_details/9.7/python-gc-traverse.md:9
msgid ""
"import gc\n"
"from vtkmodules.vtkCommonCore import vtkPoints\n"
"\n"
"class Accessor:\n"
"    def __init__(self, obj):\n"
"        self.obj = obj\n"
"\n"
"points = vtkPoints()\n"
"points.accessor = Accessor(points)  # cycle: points -> __dict__ -> accessor -"
"> points\n"
"del points\n"
"gc.collect()                        # frees both; previously freed neither\n"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../release_details/9.7/python-gc-traverse.md:23
msgid ""
"Previously the collector was told that the wrapper referred to nothing but "
"its observers, so such a cycle looked externally referenced and neither the "
"wrapper nor the C++ object it held was ever released.  Code that worked "
"around this by holding a `vtkWeakReference` remains correct."
msgstr ""
